{
  "id": "r5_fig4",
  "title": "Revenue based financing: the faster the company grows, the more the capital costs",
  "caption": "A $1 advance repaid at 8% of monthly revenue until a 1.4x cap, across three revenue growth paths. The cap never changes, so growth decides everything: a slow growing company takes 60 months and still repays only 70% of the cap, leaving the investor at -0.7% annualised, while a fast growing company clears the same cap in 36 months and hands the investor 18.8% annualised. Read from the founder's side, that means revenue based financing is cheapest for steady, predictable businesses and most expensive for the fastest growing ones, the opposite of what a growth equity investor is looking for. Terms sourced from re-cap.com and Qubit Capital. Model independently re run for this report.",
